You have access to the "2013 Boston marathon bombings" event directory from
the Han/Gao/Ciravegna augmented PHEME dataset (Zenodo DOI 10.5281/zenodo.3249977).
It contains two subfolders, `rumours/` and `non-rumours/`, each holding one
folder per tweet ID, with the tweet text inside `source-tweet/<id>.json`
(field: "text").

TASK: Read through every tweet in both `rumours/` and `non-rumours/`, and
extract every folder name tweet that relates to this specific rumor:

  THE RUMOR: the false claim that an 8-year-old GIRL, a survivor of the
  Sandy Hook Elementary School shooting, was killed in the Boston Marathon
  bombing while running the marathon in tribute to the Sandy Hook victims.
  The true fact is that the victim was an 8-year-old BOY, Martin Richard,
  a spectator (not a runner) waiting for his father to finish the race.

A tweet relates to this rumor if it does ANY of the following:
  - repeats the false claim (girl died, in any phrasing)
  - states the accurate fact as a correction/parallel to this rumor (boy,
    age 8, waiting for his father)
  - explicitly debunks or rejects the rumor ("that's fake," "it was a boy,
    not a girl," etc.)

Do NOT include rumours about the boy being killed while running for Sandy Hooks as this is still wrong and represent noise for my dataset.

Do NOT extract a tweet just because it mentions the Boston bombing
generally, a different rumor (e.g. JFK Library explosion, explosive device
counts, Westboro Baptist Church), or condolences/prayers with no reference
to the child's death.

Do not use the PHEME rumour/non-rumour folder label as a shortcut -- read
the actual tweet text. A tweet in the "non-rumours" folder can still be
extracted if it relates to this rumor, and a tweet in the "rumours" folder
should be skipped if it's about a different rumor.

OUTPUT: a plain list, one line per matching tweet:
  <tweet_id> : <full tweet text>

Process every folder in both rumours/ and non-rumours/ -- do not sample or
skip any. At the end, print the total count of extracted tweets.